                "reasoning_content": "We need to parse scenario.\n\nWe have randomized trial measuring T (treatment), M (post-treatment mediator), Y (outcome). Fully followed population: random assignment of T changed distribution of M; also changed Y. So T has total effect on Y (observed). Also M is post-treatment (i.e., after T). There is a mechanistic validation experiment: when M is interventionally fixed at same value m0 in both treatment and control groups, changing T does not change mean of Y. So holding M constant eliminates effect of T on Y. That suggests that the effect of T on Y is mediated entirely via M (at least under that experimental condition). Additionally, with T held fixed, randomly manipulating M between m0 and m1 changes mean of Y. So M has causal effect on Y (experimental evidence). Good.\n\nNow S indicates continued follow-up, is post-treatment variable, affected by both M and early measure of Y. In a complete-case analysis restricted to S=1, no difference in final Y observed between treatment groups. So conditioning on S leads to null association.\n\nAssumptions: sampling error negligible, randomization and interventions perfect, no interference. Do NOT assume absence of effect modification nor cross-world assumptions needed for natural direct effects.\n\nWe need to select statements supported.\n\nLet's evaluate each:\n\nA. \"In the fully followed population, T has a total causal effect on Y.\" We know random assignment of T changed Y. That's total causal effect (average treatment effect) present. So A is true.\n\nB. \"M has a causal effect on Y, at least under the experimentally tested conditions.\" Yes, they manipulated M while holding T fixed and saw change in Y. So B true.\n\nC. \"The average controlled direct effect when M=m0 is zero.\" Controlled direct effect (CDE) defined as effect of T on Y when M is set to a specific value (controlled). They performed experiment fixing M at m0 across groups; then changing T did not change Y. So CDE